	"nodes":[
		{"id":"d5515cb207d7771a","type":"text","text":"Jellyfish Health / Biology","x":-440,"y":-380,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"d12e25d1509ff0a7","type":"text","text":"Slow flow is still preferred. Buildings with \"windows\" allow jellyfish to control flow (?)\n\nCleaning out ammonia is important in closed spaces, which can become toxic","x":-820,"y":-435,"width":250,"height":280},
		{"id":"574954d603369dee","type":"text","text":"Different parts of jellyfish society prefers different times of day\n- Most prefer the night\n- Zooxanthellate Jellyfish prefer the day","x":-540,"y":-220,"width":250,"height":220},
		{"id":"9bfb5b3b7af57fa4","type":"text","text":"Jellyfish have gradually grown bigger thanks to more nutrients and such, making them more resistant to issues like temperature\n\nThey also have more control over their limbs, and can keep them still.\n\nThey can also see and process images, though Box Jellyfish have evolved to have superhuman vision atp","x":-220,"y":-267,"width":340,"height":367},
		{"id":"3970c84b1f2debbf","type":"text","text":"Jellies are quickly killed by very low levels of copper, zinc and other heavy metals. Lethargy and inconsistent pulse behavior in medusae can be associated with heavy metal contamination\n\nPre-treating city water with both activated carbon and reverse osmosis may be necessary to bring metal levels to non-lethal levels[^1]\n\nHeavy metal pollution is probably common in Jellyfish that have taken up residence in human cities. Constantly putting out activated carbon definitely helps with this, though jellyfish that live more nomadic styles tend to be healthier\n\n[^1]: [AZA Jellyfish Care Guide p. 22](https://assets.speakcdn.com/assets/2332/aza_jellyfish_care_manual.pdf)\n","x":-180,"y":-920,"width":380,"height":518},
		{"id":"ba5e922e5761cb09","type":"text","text":"Inspirations","x":749,"y":-555,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"#ff52bf"},
		{"id":"c960d3f92a8a9439","type":"text","text":"In 2003, MBA [Monterey Bay Aquarium] opened another very successful special exhibition featuring\njellies called “Jellies: Living Art.”[^1]\n\n[^1]: https://assets.speakcdn.com/assets/2332/aza_jellyfish_care_manual.pdf\n","x":720,"y":-460,"width":440,"height":240},
		{"id":"47986efb25bba27a","type":"text","text":"### Note; This is half science fiction and half magic-inspired. Allow yourself to make mistakes, these are for fun. Not for realism","x":380,"y":-151,"width":318,"height":191},
		{"id":"47bfbf4dcb6a9259","type":"text","text":"Jellyfish tend not to co-exist in the wild, but thanks to society they have learned to make peace with each other (though racism is still a thing)\nMoon jellyfish and other small jellyfish tend to be viewed as weak\n\nIssues:\n- Accidental stings\n- ","x":-600,"y":-920,"width":338,"height":425},
		{"id":"3e7a564b5342249d","type":"text","text":"### Hikurage\nLarge carybdeid medusa (up to 150 mm BH) lacking gastric phacellae; robust bell densely covered with nematocyst warts (Figure 7A). Morbakka virulenta possesses “rabbit-ear”-shaped rhopaliar horns and differs from its congener, M. fenneri, in that M. virulenta’s rhopaliar horns are swollen rather than pointed at the tips, and project from the top centre of the rhopaliar niche at a more oblique angle (Figure 4H versus 4I). Further, M. virulenta lacks the nematocyst wart on the rhopaliar stalk that is characteristic of M. fenneri.\nCarybdeid medusa, bell taller than wide, rectangular, with flat apex, and leaf like gonads in mature individuals (Figure 7A, B); extended tentacles flat, cord-like in mature specimens[^1]\n\n[^1]: [Carybdeid morphology p. 2613-2614\n](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/epdf/10.1080/00222933.2012.717645?needAccess=false)\n","x":-588,"y":132,"width":538,"height":468},
		{"id":"9ab16e08e68d66f9","type":"text","text":"### Diet\nMore carnivorous / venomous fish enjoy whole fish, crustaceans, mollusks, etc (Box jellies, sea nettles, lion's manes, etc)\n\nOtherwise, copepods like shrimp are highly sought after. \n\nAll jellyfish need highly unsaturated fatty acid (found in fatty fish) to be healthy. Also need polyunsaturated fatty acids (found in oysters).\n\nAlmost all jellies kept in captivity need artiermis naupilli, though wild jellies prefer wild plankton","x":-1080,"y":-45,"width":457,"height":411},
		{"id":"7e04358146d1426c","type":"text","text":"Medications are not commonly used with jellies. However, there has been successful eradication of crustacean parasites using the antiparasitic drug milbemycin oxime (Interceptor™) and with the chiton inhibitor diflubenzuron (Dimilin®). \nAttempts have been made to treat “bell rot” with tetracycline baths, yielding mixed results (see Chapter 7.6). Additional information should be\ncollected as case studies and successful treatments\n\n","x":-1120,"y":460,"width":444,"height":300},
		{"id":"ea656f907123e0bd","type":"text","text":"### Bell Rot\n\nhttps://www.vin.com/apputil/content/defaultadv1.aspx?pId=11159&meta=Generic&id=3980590\n","x":-534,"y":786,"width":250,"height":434},
		{"id":"3a4c8c87ab58f451","x":480,"y":-1340,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"5","type":"text","text":"Society"},
		{"id":"c587eea215237e5e","x":387,"y":-1265,"width":250,"height":405,"type":"text","text":"Jellyfish generally have a neutral view towards humans\n\nSome find human civilization + biology fascinating, though others will criticize their greed and whatever.\n\nJellyfish are well aware of the atrocities humans have caused"}
	],
	"edges":[]
}